<h1>Sake <em>notes</em></h1>
<p>Sake writes the type on <strong>every operation</strong>, e.g. <code>String.upcase(s)</code>. It keeps Ruby's syntax &amp; semantics where it can.</p>
<h2>Checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li>parse <strong>headings</strong> and <em>lists</em></li>
  <li>inline <code>code &lt;tags&gt;</code> stays escaped and continuation lines join</li>
  <li>links like <a href="docs/spec.md">the spec</a></li>
</ul>
<ol>
  <li>tokenize</li>
  <li>parse</li>
  <li>render</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Quotes can span two lines with a <a href="http://example.com/?a=1&b=2">link</a>.</blockquote>
<pre><code>def f(x) = x * 2   # &lt;not&gt; **markup**</code></pre>
<p>Unclosed <em>emphasis and a lone ` backtick, plus 2 </em> 3 * 4 = 24.</p>
